`AnalyticsQueryEngine` wraps an `IAnalyticsStore` and is the read entry point. Construction is trivial; the interesting surface is the query object:

```typescript
type AnalyticsQuery = {
  start: DateTime | null;
  end: DateTime | null;
  metrics: string[];
  currency: AnalyticsPath;
  select: Record<string, AnalyticsPath[]>;
  granularity: AnalyticsGranularity;
  lod: Record<string, number | null>;
};
```

`select` maps **dimension name → list of paths to match**, and `lod` maps dimension name → truncation depth. The two travel together: a path in `select` sets the *specificity* of what is matched, and the `lod` for that dimension sets how deeply results are *grouped*. Mismatch them and you get data that is technically correct and analytically useless.

`execute()` returns an array of `GroupedPeriodResult`:

```typescript
type GroupedPeriodResult = {
  period: string;
  start: DateTime;
  end: DateTime;
  rows: Array<{
    dimensions: Record<string, AnalyticsDimension>;
    metric: string;
    unit: string | null;
    value: number;
    sum: number;
  }>;
};
```

One result per time bucket, one row per unique dimension combination inside that bucket. `value` is the metric for that row in that period; `sum` is the aggregate over the whole period. `unit` is what keeps currencies from being added together — the engine never sums across units, so USD and EUR stay separate series rather than silently merging.

Practical caution from the recipe layer: the engine can infer a missing `start`/`end` from the data, but it is fragile on degenerate inputs, so pass an explicit window. And every key present in `select` needs a matching `lod` entry.

Provenance: draft `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_06-Analytics Engine/typescript/index.md`, with the explicit-window and lod-coverage caveats from the Analytics Processor recipe in `05-Lookup/00-Cookbook.md`; types confirmed against `packages/analytics-engine/core/src/` at 6.2.2-dev.45.
